+### JSON Merge Patch — `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`
+
+Applies a [JSON Merge Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386) — not a list of operations but a
+document shaped like the one it patches — and returns the merged document.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const target = {
+  title: 'Goodbye!',
+  author: { givenName: 'John', familyName: 'Doe' },
+  tags: ['example', 'sample'],
+};
+
+applyMergePatch(target, {
+  title: 'Hello!',
+  author: { familyName: null },
+  tags: ['example'],
+});
+// { title: 'Hello!', author: { givenName: 'John' }, tags: ['example'] }
+```
+
+The whole format is the recursive rule of RFC 7386, section 2, and it is worth reading as the four
+cases it is:
+
+- **An object patch against an object target is merged member by member.** A member the patch names
+  and the target has not got is added; a member both have is itself merged, so a nested object is
+  patched in place; a member the patch does not name is left as it was.
+- **A member whose value is `null` is removed**, and removing a member the target has not got is not
+  a failure, it is nothing at all. So a merge patch cannot *set* a member to `null`: section 1 says
+  the format suits documents that "do not make use of explicit null values", and a caller that needs
+  to write one wants `applyPatch`.
+- **A patch that is not an object replaces the target whole** — an array, a string, a number, a
+  boolean, and `null` too: as the patch itself, `null` is a value like any other and not a removal, so
+  `applyMergePatch(document, null)` is `null`. Arrays are values in particular, never merged element
+  by element: `{ "tags": ["example"] }` replaces the whole array, and a `null` inside one is an
+  ordinary element.
+- **A target that is not an object is ignored by an object patch**, which takes its place: merging
+  `{ "a": 1 }` into `[1, 2]`, into `"text"` or into a member that is not there all give `{ "a": 1 }`.
+  There is no member of an array or of a string for a patch to name, so nothing of the target is kept.
+
+A merge patch cannot fail. Every JSON value is a valid patch, and an object patch applies to any
+target, so there is no error for a caller to catch and no `JsonMergePatchError` to import: either the
+arguments are JSON documents and the merge has a result, or they are not JSON at all. The
+immutability contract is `applyPatch`'s: both arguments are left as they were, the result shares
+nothing with either, and a patch kept around and changed later cannot change a document already
+merged with it. Values outside the JSON data model are refused as above, by the same `TypeError`,
+which says whether it was the `target` or the `patch` that held one.
+
+What a merge patch does not say is how the members of the result are ordered; section 2 expects no
+such thing to be preserved. Compare two documents as JSON, not as text.
+
