+# applyMergePatch copies both the target and the patch, and merges into the copy
+
+## Context
+
+dl-004 settled the contract of `applyPatch`: the document handed in is copied once with `cloneJson`,
+the operations are applied to the copy, and the result shares nothing with either the document or the
+patch. `applyMergePatch` has the same obligation, but the shape of RFC 7386 makes it a different
+problem.
+
+A JSON Patch carries its new values in the `value` member of an operation, and `checkPatch` copies
+each of them out of the patch while it checks it. A merge patch has no operations: it *is* a
+document, and every part of it that is not a `null` is a value on its way into the result —
+`{ "author": { "givenName": "Jane" } }` writes that object into the target. The recursion of section
+2 writes the patch's own objects and arrays into the target as it goes, so a naive implementation
+returns a document that shares subtrees with the patch as well as with the target.
+
+It is also the first entry point of the toolkit whose *second* argument is a JSON document rather
+than a patch format, so "which argument was not JSON" is a question that did not arise before.
+
+## Decision
+
+`applyMergePatch` copies both arguments with `cloneJson`, then merges the copy of the patch into the
+copy of the target in place and returns it. The merge itself copies nothing: once both sides are
+copies, every value it writes is already one.
+
+**Both copies are taken before anything is merged, whatever the patch is.** A patch that is not an
+object replaces the target whole, so the copy of the target is thrown away in that case — and it is
+still taken, because `cloneJson` is the data-model boundary of dl-004 and the boundary does not move
+with the value of an argument. `applyMergePatch(somethingThatIsNotJson, 5)` raises rather than
+returning `5`: the caller handed in a document the toolkit cannot hold, and hearing about it should
+not depend on the patch they happened to send with it.
+
+**The result shares nothing with either argument, and neither argument is modified.** The merge
+mutates the copy of the target, never the target: a caller that keeps the patch and changes it later
+cannot change a document already merged with it, and a caller that changes the result cannot change
+the target it came from. This is dl-004's contract, extended to the patch argument because here the
+patch is where most of the new values come from.
+
+**`cloneJson`'s `TypeError` is re-thrown with the argument named.** Its message locates the refused
+value inside the value it was given — `the value at "/a/1" is …` — which does not say which of two
+documents to look in. `applyMergePatch` catches it and prefixes `the target is not a JSON value: ` or
+`the patch is not a JSON value: `, keeping the original as `cause`. That is the form `checkPatch`
+already uses for a `value` outside the data model, and it stays a `TypeError`: see dl-011.
+
+**One mutable walk, not a functional merge.** `merge(target, patch)` returns the value that stands at
+its level — the target object it has just written into, or the patch when the patch is not an object
+— so the recursion reads as the pseudocode of section 2 does. Mutation is safe because what it
+mutates is already private to the call.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The contract a caller relies on is the same for both entry points: `applyPatch` and
+  `applyMergePatch` are pure functions of their arguments, and nothing they return is entangled with
+  anything they were given.
+- A merge costs a copy of the target *and* a copy of the patch. A merge patch is normally much
+  smaller than the document it patches, so this is dl-004's cost plus a small one; the answer if it
+  ever matters is dl-004's answer, a separate entry point that shares structure, not a change to this
+  one.
+- Checking the target even when the patch replaces it means `applyMergePatch(document, patch)` has no
+  cheap path. That is deliberate: the alternative is an entry point that validates its target only
+  sometimes.
+- Nothing in the merge has to consider a value outside the JSON data model — no `undefined` member,
+  no hole in a sparse array (dl-008) — because neither copy can hold one. The merge is the algorithm
+  of section 2 and nothing else.
