+# JSON Merge Patch has no error hierarchy: no merge patch can be invalid
+
+## Context
+
+Every building block of the toolkit so far publishes error classes: dl-001 gave JSON Pointer three,
+dl-005 gave JSON Patch four, and in both cases the classes exist because the feature has distinct
+failures a caller does different things about. Symmetry suggests a `JsonMergePatchError` with an
+`InvalidMergePatchError` under it, so that the three entry points look alike.
+
+They would have nothing to report. RFC 7386 has no shape for a patch to violate: section 2 says "The
+Target argument can be any JSON value or undefined. The Patch argument can be any JSON value", and
+the algorithm has no error branch — an object patch merges into an object, and against any other
+target it simply replaces it; a `null` removes a member, and removes nothing at all if the member is
+absent; a patch that is not an object replaces the target whole. There is no location that has to
+exist, as `remove` and `replace` need one in RFC 6902, no pointer syntax to parse, no precondition to
+fail. A merge patch always has a result.
+
+What is left is the one failure dl-004 already defines: an argument that is not a JSON value at all —
+`undefined`, a function, `NaN`, a `Date`, a cycle, the hole of a sparse array (dl-008).
+
+## Decision
+
+`src/json-merge-patch.ts` publishes `applyMergePatch` and no error class. The only throw is the
+`TypeError` of dl-004, raised by `cloneJson` for either argument and re-thrown with the argument
+named (dl-010).
+
+**No class is published for a condition the types already rule out.** dl-004 decided that a document
+outside the JSON data model is a programming error on the caller's side, not a patch that failed, and
+kept it a `TypeError` outside the `JsonPatchError` hierarchy. Both arguments of `applyMergePatch` are
+documents, so that reasoning covers both of them, and nothing else can go wrong. A
+`JsonMergePatchError` would be a public class with one member and no caller: dl-005 notes that moving
+a condition between error classes is a breaking change, and a class nobody can usefully catch is a
+commitment taken for nothing.
+
+**The patch is not treated as external input in the way a JSON Patch is.** `checkPatch` reports a
+`value` outside the data model as `InvalidPatchError` because a JSON Patch has a format, and a
+request body that does not meet it is a malformed request the service answers for. A merge patch has
+no format: a request body that parses as JSON *is* a valid merge patch. The only thing a service can
+hand `applyMergePatch` that is not a merge patch is something `JSON.parse` never produced — and that
+is a bug in the service, which is exactly what a `TypeError` says.
+
+**The documentation carries the weight instead.** The README states that a merge patch cannot fail
+and that there is no error to import, because a caller coming from `applyPatch` will look for the
+error list and has to be told that the absence is the design, not an omission.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A caller merges without a `try`/`catch`: `applyMergePatch(document, patch)` either returns a
+  document or reveals a bug in the calling code. A service validating a `PATCH` body needs only to
+  parse it.
+- The two entry points are deliberately asymmetric, and the README says why. A reader who expects
+  `JsonMergePatchError` by analogy will not find one.
+- If a later feature does give merge patches a failure mode of their own — a size or depth limit, a
+  schema check — it will need an error class, and introducing one then is a compatible addition,
+  whereas removing an unused one would not have been.
+- `TypeError` stays the toolkit's one answer for "this is not a JSON value", in all three building
+  blocks. A caller that wants to guard against it guards once, around any of them.
