+
+# JSON Merge Patch scope: strict RFC 7386, as RFC 7396 corrects it
+
+## Context
+
+dl-002 and dl-006 settled the points RFC 6901 and RFC 6902 leave to the reader, once, in favour of
+the RFC. RFC 7386 is shorter than either — one page of pseudocode — but its brevity hides the same
+kind of choices, and merge patch implementations in the wild differ on several of them: some merge
+arrays element by element, some let a patch write an explicit `null`, some refuse a patch that is not
+an object. The toolkit is imported by several of the team's services, so a merge patch has to mean
+the same thing everywhere.
+
+There is also a question about which document to implement. The request names RFC 7386; RFC 7396
+carries the same title, the same authors and the same date, and says only "Obsoletes: 7386". A diff
+of the two texts shows the whole difference: RFC 7386 was published with the indentation of its
+examples and of the section 2 pseudocode lost, so that the body of the `for` loop and the
+`Target = {}` line appear at the same level as the statements they belong under. RFC 7396 restores
+it. The algorithm, the prose and the appendix A test cases are identical, character for character.
+
+## Decision
+
+The implementation follows section 2 as RFC 7396 prints it, which is RFC 7386's algorithm with its
+nesting readable. The module's doc comment says so, so that nobody has to re-run that diff. Each
+point below is settled in favour of the RFC and covered by a test; the fifteen test cases of appendix
+A are a test table of their own.
+
+**`null` removes a member, and nothing else can.** A member of an object patch whose value is `null`
+removes the member of that name from the target, and removing a member the target has not got is not
+a failure — the algorithm's `if Name exists in Target` simply does not fire. The consequence is the
+one section 1 states: there is no way to *set* a member to `null`. That is not a gap to work around
+with a sentinel of our own; a caller that needs an explicit `null` has `applyPatch`, and the README
+says so where the surprise is.
+
+**A patch that is not an object replaces the target whole, `null` included.** `null` is a removal
+only as the value of a member of an object patch; as the patch itself it is an ordinary JSON value,
+so `applyMergePatch(document, null)` is `null` and not an emptied or deleted document. The
+pseudocode's final `else: return Patch` is unconditional, and reading the top-level `null` as "remove
+everything" would be an invention.
+
+**Arrays are values, never merged.** An array in a patch replaces the array, object or scalar it
+lands on, whole; an array target is ignored by an object patch rather than indexed by its member
+names, so merging `{ "1": "z" }` into `["x", "y"]` gives the object `{ "1": "z" }`. Section 2 is
+explicit: "it is not possible to patch part of a target that is not an object, such as to replace
+just some of the values in an array". A `null` inside an array is an ordinary element, since the
+array is a value and not a patch. This is the format's real limitation, and the reason RFC 6902
+exists alongside it.
+
+**A target that is not an object is ignored, not merged into.** `Target = {}` discards it: an object
+patch against `5`, `"text"`, `null`, an array, or a member the target has not got all merge into a
+fresh empty object. So an object patch of nothing but `null`s against a non-object target gives `{}`
+— appendix A's `[1,2]` with `{"a":"b","c":null}` giving `{"a":"b"}` is this case.
+
+**A member the target has not got is the "undefined" target of section 2.** The recursive call is
+`MergePatch(Target[Name], Value)`, and section 2 says the target argument "can be any JSON value or
+undefined". The merge passes `undefined` for a member the object has not got, which the first rule
+above turns into `{}`, so `{}` merged with `{"a":{"bb":{"ccc":null}}}` gives `{"a":{"bb":{}}}`:
+objects are created along the way, and the `null`s inside them still remove nothing. This is the
+opposite of dl-006's rule for JSON Patch, which creates no location along the way — and both are
+right, because `add` names a location with a pointer while a merge patch *is* the shape of the
+document.
+
+**`undefined` as a patch member is refused, not read as a removal.** `{ a: undefined }` in
+JavaScript is a mistake next to `{ a: null }`, and reading it as a removal would hide it. It is a
+value outside the JSON data model, so dl-004's `TypeError` refuses it, as dl-008 refuses the hole of
+a sparse array for the same reason.
+
+**Member names are names, not pointers.** A merge patch has the shape of its target, so `"/a/b"` is a
+member called `/a/b` and `"~1"` is a member called `~1`. There is no escaping and no pointer syntax
+anywhere in RFC 7386; the empty string is an ordinary member name as it is in dl-002.
+
+**Member names that mean something to JavaScript are ordinary member names, as in dl-006.** Writes go
+through `setMember`, so a patch can give a document a `__proto__` member without changing the
+prototype of anything; reads go through `Object.hasOwn`, so a patch member named `toString` has
+nothing inherited to merge into and is written as a new own member, and a `null` under that name
+removes nothing. Prototype pollution through a merged document is closed off the same way as in
+dl-006: by construction, with no name treated specially.
+
+**The order of the result's members is not part of the contract.** Section 2 expects no preservation
+of "white space, member ordering, number precision". The merge happens to keep the target's members
+in place and append the patch's new ones in the order the patch lists them, which is deterministic,
+but no test asserts it and no caller should rely on it. Documents are compared as JSON, not as text.
+
+**Duplicate member names are out of scope**, as they were in dl-006: `applyMergePatch` takes a parsed
+document, in which JSON's last-one-wins rule has already resolved them. Section 2 says the result on
+such objects "is not defined"; whatever parses the request body is where that would be caught.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A merge patch that applies here applies the same way in every service, and the points implementations
+  differ on — array merging, explicit `null`, a non-object patch — behave as the RFC says rather than
+  as convenience would suggest.
+- Callers migrating from an implementation that merges arrays will see arrays replaced. That is the
+  format: a patch that needs to change one element of an array is a JSON Patch.
+- The two patch formats now sit side by side in the toolkit with opposite answers on creating a path
+  and on writing a `null`. Both answers are their own RFC's, and the README states each next to its
+  entry point, so the choice between the formats is the caller's to make with the facts in hand.
+- Nothing in the implementation is specific to RFC 7386 over RFC 7396: if a later document supersedes
+  them with the same algorithm, only the reference in the doc comment changes.
