+
+# Implement RFC 7386 JSON Merge Patch applyMergePatch
+
+## Request
+
+Some of our clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format: implement JSON Merge
+Patch as specified in RFC 7386, exporting `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It
+applies a merge patch to a target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test-first, red before green: `test/json-merge-patch.test.ts` was written and run against a missing
+`applyMergePatch` — `SyntaxError: The requested module '../src/index.ts' does not provide an export
+named 'applyMergePatch'` — then `src/json-merge-patch.ts` was added until all 57 cases passed. The
+182 cases of task-001 and task-002 were kept green throughout.
+
+Both RFC 7386 and RFC 7396, which obsoletes it, were read. Their texts differ only in indentation:
+RFC 7386 was published with the nesting of its examples and of the section 2 pseudocode lost. The
+algorithm, the prose and the fifteen appendix A test cases are identical, so the implementation
+follows RFC 7386 as RFC 7396 prints it, and the module's doc comment records that so nobody has to
+re-run the diff.
+
+### Code
+
+- `src/json-merge-patch.ts` — `applyMergePatch`, the recursive `merge` of RFC 7386, section 2, and
+  two helpers: the own-member read that makes an absent member the section's "undefined" target, and
+  the copy that names which argument was not a JSON value.
+- `src/index.ts` — publishes `applyMergePatch`, next to what task-001 and task-002 published.
+
+Nothing else changed: the new module needs only `cloneJson` and `setMember` from `json-document.ts`
+and `isJsonObject` from `json-value.ts`, which is dl-007's layout working as intended — a merge patch
+names no location with a pointer, so it does not touch `json-pointer.ts`, and it publishes no error
+class, so it does not touch `json-patch-errors.ts`.
+
+`applyMergePatch` copies the target and the patch, merges the copy of the patch into the copy of the
+target in place, and returns it: an object patch against an object target is merged member by member,
+a member whose value is `null` is removed, a patch that is not an object replaces the target whole,
+and a target that is not an object is ignored by an object patch.
+
+### Tests
+
+`test/json-merge-patch.test.ts`, 57 cases: the fifteen example test cases of appendix A as a table;
+the examples of sections 1 and 3; the four cases of the algorithm, each with its happy path and its
+edges — nested merge, a member created from an absent target, an empty patch, the empty-string member
+name, member names that look like JSON Pointers; `null` as a removal, as an array element and as the
+whole patch, and the member it cannot set; arrays replaced whole rather than merged; both arguments
+left alone and sharing nothing with the result, including when the same document is passed as both;
+values outside the JSON data model in either argument, with the message naming which one;
+`{ a: undefined }` refused rather than read as a removal; and `__proto__` and inherited names as
+ordinary member names.
+
+### Documentation
+
+`README.md` gained a "JSON Merge Patch" building block, after "JSON Patch": the example of RFC 7386,
+section 3, the format as the four cases of the algorithm, the explicit `null` it gives up and what to
+use instead, the absence of any error to catch, and the member ordering that is not part of the
+contract.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- dl-010 — both arguments are copied and the merge happens in the copy, so the result shares nothing
+  with either; dl-004's contract extended to the patch argument, where a merge patch's values come
+  from.
+- dl-011 — no error hierarchy: RFC 7386 gives a merge patch no way to be invalid and no operation that
+  can fail, so the only throw is dl-004's `TypeError` for an argument that is not a JSON value.
+- dl-012 — the strict reading of RFC 7386: `null` removes and cannot be written, a non-object patch
+  replaces the target whole, arrays are values, a non-object target is ignored, objects are created
+  along the way, member names are names and not pointers, and `__proto__` is an ordinary member name.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 239 tests, 239 pass, 0 fail (57 for JSON Merge Patch, 182 for the two earlier blocks).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, `applyMergePatch` included.
+- `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json --noUnusedLocals --noUnusedParameters` reports nothing.
+- The README's example and the module's doc-comment example were run as written, and produce the
+  documented results.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- The follow-ups of task-001 and task-002 stand: no linter, and `package.json` still declares no
+  `main` or `exports`, so the published surface of `src/index.ts` is not yet enforced by the package
+  itself.
+- `dist/*.d.ts` keeps the `.ts` import extensions that `dist/*.js` has rewritten to `.js`. It
+  predates this task and does not affect the emitted JavaScript, but a consumer type-checking against
+  `dist/` would trip on it; worth a look when the `exports` map above is added.
+- A merge patch cannot express an explicit `null`, by design. If a service needs one, the answer is
+  `applyPatch`; if that turns out to be common, a converter from a merge patch to the JSON Patch that
+  has the same effect would be the thing to add, not a dialect of merge patch.
